1
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:45,070
(MUEZZIN SINGS CALL TO PRAYER)

2
00:00:55,830 --> 00:00:58,300
(WOMAN WAILS)

3
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:00,260
(HELICOPTER WHIRRING)

4
00:01:00,310 --> 00:01:02,030
(COUGHING)

5
00:01:10,397 --> 00:01:14,464
- You're gonna be all right, mate.
- They're in there! Others.

6
00:01:33,677 --> 00:01:35,907
(SHOUTING)

7
00:01:37,237 --> 00:01:40,237
They're in there!

8
00:01:45,437 --> 00:01:47,997
Clear! Clear!

9
00:01:48,037 --> 00:01:51,157
(BREAKING GLASS)

10
00:01:51,197 --> 00:01:54,157
Clear!

11
00:02:14,237 --> 00:02:17,704
- This one's still alive. Get a medic!
- (MAN SHOUTS)

12
00:02:19,597 --> 00:02:21,557
(MAN SHOUTS)

13
00:02:25,477 --> 00:02:27,157
(VEHICLE SOUNDS HORN)

14
00:02:27,197 --> 00:02:30,276
In this country, although we pride
ourselves on our freedom of speech,

15
00:02:30,277 --> 00:02:32,837
it is not an enshrined right.

16
00:02:32,877 --> 00:02:36,347
Now, Shelby Brookes
was correctly convicted

17
00:02:36,397 --> 00:02:40,196
for stirring up hatred against
persons on religious grounds,

18
00:02:40,197 --> 00:02:44,317
contrary to the Public Order Act 1986,
Section 29 (b),

19
00:02:44,357 --> 00:02:48,987
as inserted by the Racial
and Religious Hatred Act 2006.

20
00:02:49,037 --> 00:02:52,507
The evidence presented at this trial

21
00:02:52,557 --> 00:02:56,837
clearly demonstrates
that he intended to stir up such hatred.

22
00:02:57,837 --> 00:03:02,387
And in one particularly offensive passage,
and I quote,

23
00:03:02,437 --> 00:03:04,797
"Islam is a backward religion.

24
00:03:04,837 --> 00:03:07,236
"It's highly dangerous and intolerant.

25
00:03:07,237 --> 00:03:10,317
"Everyone knows it,
but we're becoming a state

26
00:03:10,357 --> 00:03:13,916
"where ordinary British people
are scared to take action..."

27
00:03:13,917 --> 00:03:17,387
- (MURMURING)
- "...like proper citizens." Unquote.

28
00:03:17,437 --> 00:03:19,756
Now, these words may seem harmless,

29
00:03:19,757 --> 00:03:21,996
but spoken at the end
of a rally in Bradford,

30
00:03:21,997 --> 00:03:24,837
they were deliberately provocative

31
00:03:24,877 --> 00:03:28,637
- and led to rioting.
- (WHISPERING)

32
00:03:28,677 --> 00:03:30,507
(CHATTER)

33
00:03:30,557 --> 00:03:34,437
I'm Sir Ian Rochester,
Permanent Secretary of the DCA.

34
00:03:34,477 --> 00:03:39,107
Now, I ask you to give proper weight
to the statutory protections,

35
00:03:39,157 --> 00:03:41,547
as inserted by 29 (j) of the Act.

36
00:03:41,597 --> 00:03:44,637
This guarantees that none
of the provisions

37
00:03:44,677 --> 00:03:46,716
to which Sir Alan
has directed Your Lordships

38
00:03:46,717 --> 00:03:50,836
can ever be interpreted in such a way
as to prohibit or restrict discussion,

39
00:03:50,837 --> 00:03:54,516
criticism, ridicule or even abuse
of particular religions

40
00:03:54,517 --> 00:03:58,637
or the beliefs of their adherents.

41
00:03:58,677 --> 00:04:02,677
Attacking an individual,
that is a different matter.

42
00:04:02,717 --> 00:04:05,027
Shelby Brookes didn't do that.

43
00:04:05,077 --> 00:04:08,196
He has a protected right,
like every other person,

44
00:04:08,197 --> 00:04:12,317
to challenge or condemn
any belief system or religion.

45
00:04:12,357 --> 00:04:18,197
Despite legislation put in place
to protect his right to criticise ideas,

46
00:04:18,237 --> 00:04:21,707
the police chose to be provocative
in arresting him.

47
00:04:21,757 --> 00:04:28,107
So, unless and until we proscribe
Mr Brookes's party,

48
00:04:28,157 --> 00:04:35,397
we must defend to our dying breath
his right to speak freely, within the law,

49
00:04:35,437 --> 00:04:37,557
regardless of who that offends.

50
00:04:37,597 --> 00:04:39,957
Thank you, Mrs Mills.

51
00:04:39,997 --> 00:04:43,716
Would you defend your client's
right to deny the Holocaust?

52
00:04:43,717 --> 00:04:46,997
He made such a statement
on one occasion.

53
00:04:47,037 --> 00:04:51,907
As offensive as that idea is, the law
must defend his right to express it.

54
00:04:51,957 --> 00:04:55,747
- The alternative is dictatorship.
- Hm.

55
00:04:55,797 --> 00:04:58,797
We shall adjourn to consider ourjudgment.

56
00:04:58,837 --> 00:05:01,197
All rise.

57
00:05:06,997 --> 00:05:08,477
Good morning?

58
00:05:08,517 --> 00:05:11,956
Well, I should feel more sanguine about it
if Deed wasn't on the bench.

59
00:05:11,957 --> 00:05:15,556
- Did you see the mob outside?
- And why the police allow it...

60
00:05:15,557 --> 00:05:19,596
Oh, yes, the Cabinet's braced for trouble
if this... if this appeal succeeds.

61
00:05:19,597 --> 00:05:21,276
Deed won't let that influence him.

62
00:05:21,277 --> 00:05:23,316
I thought he'd been
sent off to The Hague.

63
00:05:23,317 --> 00:05:26,156
There's no work for him there.
He's at liberty to sit back here.

64
00:05:26,157 --> 00:05:28,197
Mrs Mills, your feelings?

65
00:05:28,237 --> 00:05:30,276
I think it's shameful
the Government ever brought this case.

66
00:05:30,277 --> 00:05:31,877
What else could I expect

67
00:05:31,917 --> 00:05:36,184
from that radical bunch of loonies
that she's joined forces with?

68
00:05:37,117 --> 00:05:39,877
His opinions are odious,

69
00:05:39,917 --> 00:05:44,316
but in a democratic society, is it
really necessary that we zip his mouth?

70
00:05:44,317 --> 00:05:48,156
Well, Article 10 of the European
Convention forbids us to do so.

71
00:05:48,157 --> 00:05:50,796
Not entirely.
It restricts his rights sometimes,

72
00:05:50,797 --> 00:05:54,676
for public safety and for the protection
of the rights of others.

73
00:05:54,677 --> 00:05:56,117
Brookes is a racist.

74
00:05:56,157 --> 00:05:59,837
He hates blacks and Muslims
and gays and Jews,

75
00:05:59,877 --> 00:06:01,916
and people with six toes, for all I know.

76
00:06:01,917 --> 00:06:04,356
And, despite the location of his speech,

77
00:06:04,357 --> 00:06:07,396
Brookes was careful
to attack only the religion.

78
00:06:07,397 --> 00:06:11,717
Can we permit a religious creed
to be immune from criticism

79
00:06:11,757 --> 00:06:13,667
simply because its adherents

80
00:06:13,717 --> 00:06:17,556
- claim that that criticism is insulting?
- (CLOCK CHIMING)

81
00:06:17,557 --> 00:06:20,756
We have to try to get
behind the words, Joe, to intent.

82
00:06:20,757 --> 00:06:22,836
Wasn't Islam being held up in hatred?

83
00:06:22,837 --> 00:06:29,157
There is European jurisprudence,
Jersild versus Denmark 1955.

84
00:06:29,197 --> 00:06:34,147
Ajournalist was convicted
of aiding and abetting racial hatred.

85
00:06:34,197 --> 00:06:36,917
He had interviewed three neo-Nazis.

86
00:06:36,957 --> 00:06:39,107
On overturning the conviction,

87
00:06:39,157 --> 00:06:43,627
the court ruled that it would
seriously hamper press freedom

88
00:06:43,677 --> 00:06:46,236
were he prevented
from disseminating such views.

89
00:06:46,237 --> 00:06:49,707
JOHN: This still ignores Article 17.

90
00:06:49,757 --> 00:06:53,116
We can't interpret any right
in the Convention in any way

91
00:06:53,117 --> 00:06:55,836
that allows Brookes to destroy
any of its other rights and freedoms.

92
00:06:55,837 --> 00:06:57,957
Brookes was following the law

93
00:06:57,997 --> 00:07:02,036
by not attacking the security
of the Muslims under the Convention,

94
00:07:02,037 --> 00:07:04,187
only their ideas.

95
00:07:04,237 --> 00:07:06,676
Ideas lead to physical reality,
Joe, violence.

96
00:07:06,677 --> 00:07:09,036
I can't believe
that Brookes wasn't aware of that.

97
00:07:09,037 --> 00:07:12,556
There's very little we can do about it
under the law as it now stands.

98
00:07:12,557 --> 00:07:19,747
In a full democracy, ideas alone
don't undermine anyone's peace, John.

99
00:07:19,797 --> 00:07:23,596
- Do we live in a full democracy?
- That's why we need these protections.

100
00:07:23,597 --> 00:07:28,116
How would you feel if your core religious
beliefs were held up for ridicule

101
00:07:28,117 --> 00:07:31,356
by somebody claiming
to represent the white majority?

102
00:07:31,357 --> 00:07:33,827
I'd want the blasphemer stoned.

103
00:07:33,877 --> 00:07:37,076
We'll all be stoned if we overturn
the lower court and release him.

104
00:07:37,077 --> 00:07:38,957
Now, there's a dilemma.

105
00:07:38,997 --> 00:07:42,596
Should we uphold a wrong judgment
for fear of reprisals?

106
00:07:42,597 --> 00:07:45,397
I'm still not convinced it was wrong.

107
00:07:45,437 --> 00:07:51,757
Then we must each take our own counsel
and meet at a later date. 14 days?

108
00:07:51,797 --> 00:07:53,787
(SIREN WAILING)

109
00:07:56,157 --> 00:07:58,467
11 people shot in Basra

110
00:07:58,517 --> 00:08:01,836
is hardly huge in relation to the number
of people killed there, Ian.

111
00:08:01,837 --> 00:08:05,596
These were civilians killed by a British
soldier. Four of them were children.

112
00:08:05,597 --> 00:08:09,316
- Well, three were terrorists, surely?
- That was the Army's original position.

113
00:08:09,317 --> 00:08:12,036
The Iraqi government
now claims none were.

114
00:08:12,037 --> 00:08:15,636
- Huh! Well, how reliable is that?
- They want the case heard in Iraq.

115
00:08:15,637 --> 00:08:19,036
Well, the PM might go along with it
as part of our exit strategy.

116
00:08:19,037 --> 00:08:20,867
Pity poor Private Clark.

117
00:08:20,917 --> 00:08:23,067
Have we got an exit strategy?

118
00:08:24,797 --> 00:08:27,476
- The Army wants a court martial.
- Well, they won't get it.

119
00:08:27,477 --> 00:08:32,036
Not even with the Secretary of State
for Defence arguing for it in Cabinet.

120
00:08:32,037 --> 00:08:33,517
Taxi!

121
00:08:33,557 --> 00:08:36,077
Er... Garrick Club, please.

122
00:08:37,837 --> 00:08:39,827
(VEHICLE SOUNDS HORN)

123
00:08:40,917 --> 00:08:42,907
Sir Joseph.

124
00:08:55,237 --> 00:08:57,197
(ALARM SOUNDS)

125
00:08:57,237 --> 00:09:00,756
- Did you recognise the woman, Joe?
- Well, do you think it's serious?

126
00:09:00,757 --> 00:09:03,396
The Home Office can't treat it
as anything other than serious.

127
00:09:03,397 --> 00:09:05,036
But I barely saw the woman.

128
00:09:05,037 --> 00:09:07,476
You'll all have to have
round-the-clock protection.

129
00:09:07,477 --> 00:09:10,517
(LAUGHS) As long as she's got good legs!

130
00:09:10,557 --> 00:09:12,357
Ooh, yeah!

131
00:09:17,557 --> 00:09:19,597
(BIRDSONG OUTSIDE)

132
00:09:59,357 --> 00:10:01,557
What are Sir John's routines?

133
00:10:01,597 --> 00:10:04,916
I keep his diary quite secure.
You'll have to ask him.

134
00:10:04,917 --> 00:10:06,917
This is not helping your boss.

135
00:10:06,957 --> 00:10:11,076
I'm beginning to think this is a move
by the Lord Chancellor to restrict me.

136
00:10:11,077 --> 00:10:14,116
We can't take any risks.
We're taking it very seriously, sir.

137
00:10:14,117 --> 00:10:16,156
It was Sir Joe Channing
that was threatened.

138
00:10:16,157 --> 00:10:19,196
- Yeah, I've been assigned to your staff.
- I don't think Mrs Cooper needs any help.

139
00:10:19,197 --> 00:10:22,236
I'm sure you wouldn't like it if Mrs
Cooper opened a letter addressed to you,

140
00:10:22,237 --> 00:10:23,837
and it exploded.

141
00:10:23,877 --> 00:10:27,397
I wouldn't like it any better
if it happened to you.

142
00:10:28,437 --> 00:10:31,236
All the while you're here,
you must report to Mrs Cooper.

143
00:10:31,237 --> 00:10:33,196
- Sir, I really think...
- Yes, yes, all right.

144
00:10:33,197 --> 00:10:37,331
I'm sure you're very good at what you do,
but so is Mrs Cooper.

145
00:10:42,997 --> 00:10:44,996
- Is he always so difficult?
- Huh!

146
00:10:44,997 --> 00:10:48,307
- I will need to know his movements.
- I wish I knew them myself.

147
00:10:48,308 --> 00:10:51,476
- He has lots of girlfriends.
- I wouldn't know about that.

148
00:10:51,477 --> 00:10:53,636
Is he sleeping
with the barrister, Mrs Mills?

149
00:10:53,637 --> 00:10:57,276
- Is that anyone's business but his?
- He could be at risk.

150
00:10:57,277 --> 00:11:00,689
Look, we have to ensure his safety
without his knowing.

151
00:11:00,690 --> 00:11:03,329
He's in court on another appeal
this afternoon.

152
00:11:03,330 --> 00:11:06,609
Is it likely to attract
a lot of the general public?

153
00:11:06,610 --> 00:11:09,529
The status of electronic bank drafts
in the law of theft?

154
00:11:09,530 --> 00:11:11,889
Yeah, they'll be queuing
down The Strand(!)

155
00:11:11,890 --> 00:11:16,024
Afterwards, he's meeting Mrs Mills.
They're fencing at the gym.

156
00:11:21,770 --> 00:11:26,850
I don't care how stable a trial in Baghdad
makes those rag-heads look...!

157
00:11:26,890 --> 00:11:29,729
Sounds like the Secretary of State
for Defence is adamant, Percy.

158
00:11:29,730 --> 00:11:31,409
Ready to punch someone, I'd say.

159
00:11:31,410 --> 00:11:33,450
...Our boys deserve better!

160
00:11:33,490 --> 00:11:36,849
The proper course is for the Army
to court-martial Private Clark.

161
00:11:36,850 --> 00:11:38,329
The Iraqis won't budge.

162
00:11:38,330 --> 00:11:41,969
- We wrest the country back from a madman.
- (CLOCK CHIMING)

163
00:11:41,970 --> 00:11:46,009
- Now they imagine they're a democracy.
- More people voted in Iraq than here.

164
00:11:46,010 --> 00:11:47,809
Well, you know the PM's view, Martin.

165
00:11:47,810 --> 00:11:51,209
It'll help stabilise Iraq if the courts
are at least seen to be functioning.

166
00:11:51,210 --> 00:11:54,409
But the troops on the ground would feel
they have no protection until we exit.

167
00:11:54,410 --> 00:11:57,449
The longer we delay the functioning
of their administration,

168
00:11:57,450 --> 00:12:00,529
- the longer that exit will take, General.
- (CLOCK TICKING)

169
00:12:00,530 --> 00:12:03,929
(SNORTS) A trial in Iraq
would be seen as a sellout.

170
00:12:03,930 --> 00:12:06,264
Even more of our lads would desert.

171
00:12:06,290 --> 00:12:09,050
No-one's selling them out, Andy.

172
00:12:09,090 --> 00:12:12,290
I'll fight this one in public
if necessary.

173
00:12:12,330 --> 00:12:14,369
See what the electorate thinks.

174
00:12:14,370 --> 00:12:17,090
(SWORDS TAP AND SCRAPE NEXT DOOR)

175
00:12:21,210 --> 00:12:23,650
(SIGHS)

176
00:12:23,690 --> 00:12:26,489
The police tramped
through my apartment today.

177
00:12:26,490 --> 00:12:29,009
Checking on security
in case you visit me.

178
00:12:29,010 --> 00:12:30,920
That was a bit presumptuous.

179
00:12:31,659 --> 00:12:33,938
I think it provided them with an excuse.

180
00:12:33,939 --> 00:12:37,218
Because of my relationship
with Radical Lawyers for Justice.

181
00:12:37,219 --> 00:12:40,698
The police have become an arm
of government. Nobody seems to care.

182
00:12:40,699 --> 00:12:44,218
Well, you'd better start caring. You might
need to rely on them for your security.

183
00:12:44,219 --> 00:12:46,689
I can't take that seriously.

184
00:12:49,819 --> 00:12:52,459
They did ask about us.

185
00:12:52,499 --> 00:12:55,059
What did you tell 'em?

186
00:12:55,099 --> 00:12:58,379
- That I can never be with you.
- Why?

187
00:12:59,459 --> 00:13:02,258
What's that all about?
I thought life was good.

188
00:13:02,259 --> 00:13:05,139
Yeah. It is for you, John.

189
00:13:06,659 --> 00:13:09,338
It's like you can't be touched
on any level.

190
00:13:09,339 --> 00:13:11,098
Is this why you've hooked up
with Radical Lawyers?

191
00:13:11,099 --> 00:13:13,329
No, that was a good offer.

192
00:13:13,379 --> 00:13:16,178
In legal circles, it's self-immolation.
You could have been a judge.

193
00:13:16,179 --> 00:13:18,618
Yeah, and then I'd be unassailable too,
and I don't want that.

194
00:13:18,619 --> 00:13:21,138
Jo, come on.
Why are you doing this? Don't do this.

195
00:13:21,139 --> 00:13:23,618
Because I don't like the way
I'm living my life, John.

196
00:13:23,619 --> 00:13:25,738
- I want to change it.
- Is this helping?

197
00:13:25,739 --> 00:13:27,698
Excuse me, but who are you
to question that?

198
00:13:27,699 --> 00:13:30,258
If you get close to this lot,
you'll never be a judge.

199
00:13:30,259 --> 00:13:32,418
What difference could I make
on the bench, anyway?

200
00:13:32,419 --> 00:13:35,218
Mm-hm? And if you're stopped
from being a barrister?

201
00:13:35,219 --> 00:13:37,579
Has anyone threatened that?

202
00:13:37,619 --> 00:13:40,699
This new set is totally lopsided,

203
00:13:40,739 --> 00:13:44,819
totally political.
They ignore all principles of objectivity.

204
00:13:44,859 --> 00:13:47,298
No, they believe in what they're doing,

205
00:13:47,299 --> 00:13:50,538
and that is more than can be said
for most lawyers.

206
00:13:50,539 --> 00:13:54,739
Don't you use them
as a stick to beat me with.

207
00:13:54,779 --> 00:13:57,419
We've got too much history for that.

208
00:13:57,459 --> 00:14:00,459
And don't you manipulate me, John.

209
00:14:02,579 --> 00:14:05,138
I can't talk to you any more.
I can't reach you.

210
00:14:05,139 --> 00:14:09,529
You just... you just take a position
and you don't shift.

211
00:14:09,579 --> 00:14:12,299
A bomb is probably all that'll move you.

212
00:14:13,339 --> 00:14:15,329
(DOOR CLOSES)

213
00:14:16,539 --> 00:14:18,579
(BIRDSONG OUTSIDE)

214
00:14:47,379 --> 00:14:49,339
(BELL RINGS)

215
00:15:14,539 --> 00:15:16,579
It is an outrage.

216
00:15:16,619 --> 00:15:18,378
The British are denying us justice

217
00:15:18,379 --> 00:15:21,846
by refusing to allow us
to try Private Clark in Iraq.

218
00:15:21,859 --> 00:15:24,498
Instead, they are insisting on The Hague.

219
00:15:24,499 --> 00:15:26,538
Yeah, I've been following the public row

220
00:15:26,539 --> 00:15:28,658
between the British PM
and the Army Minister.

221
00:15:28,659 --> 00:15:31,298
This soldier must be tried on Iraqi soil.

222
00:15:31,299 --> 00:15:35,738
That might be risky, Mr Ambassador.
It suggests you want a lynch party.

223
00:15:35,739 --> 00:15:37,809
We want only justice.

224
00:15:37,859 --> 00:15:41,898
But The Hague is the perfect venue.
Politically sophisticated.

225
00:15:41,899 --> 00:15:46,819
Yes, but can the International
Criminal Court deliverjustice for Iraq?

226
00:15:46,859 --> 00:15:51,409
I will prosecute personally.
Let's talk to Claude Previn.

227
00:15:51,459 --> 00:15:54,539
It is still a trial in English
for the English.

228
00:15:54,579 --> 00:16:00,099
Given France's consistent opposition
to the war, you need have no concerns.

229
00:16:00,139 --> 00:16:05,419
Iraq must make a formal request
under the Statute of Rome, Article 13.

230
00:16:06,859 --> 00:16:09,898
We would like this case, monsieur.
N'est-ce pas, Marie?

231
00:16:09,899 --> 00:16:12,858
We would make sure
the soldier was found guilty.

232
00:16:12,859 --> 00:16:14,914
The consequences of their illegal war.

233
00:16:14,915 --> 00:16:17,578
- Claude?
- If only we could fight that battle.

234
00:16:17,579 --> 00:16:20,339
I will talk... to our Justice Minister.

235
00:16:20,379 --> 00:16:21,818
As far as the Army's concerned,

236
00:16:21,819 --> 00:16:24,418
the International Criminal Court
is little better than Iraq.

237
00:16:24,419 --> 00:16:25,819
Hardly, General.

238
00:16:25,859 --> 00:16:28,298
The soldiers on the ground
won't notice any difference.

239
00:16:28,299 --> 00:16:30,778
And how many more desertions
can we sustain?

240
00:16:30,779 --> 00:16:33,009
3,000 to date.

241
00:16:33,059 --> 00:16:37,298
It's out of our hands now. We are official
signatories to the ICC, after all.

242
00:16:37,299 --> 00:16:39,018
Some means must be found to block this.

243
00:16:39,019 --> 00:16:42,378
If we don't, soldiers who don't go AWOL
will avoid taking action

244
00:16:42,379 --> 00:16:44,378
for fear of being made
a political football.

245
00:16:44,379 --> 00:16:47,419
This is part of our exit strategy.

246
00:16:47,459 --> 00:16:50,769
- (CLOCK TICKING)
- What exit strategy?

247
00:16:52,059 --> 00:16:56,659
Well, I, er... I suppose we must
get out sooner rather than later.

248
00:16:57,779 --> 00:17:01,658
Then we must pre-empt a trial at The Hague
and try him by court martial.

249
00:17:01,659 --> 00:17:04,178
Well, that would cause
an international incident.

250
00:17:04,179 --> 00:17:06,859
There might be a simpler solution.

251
00:17:06,899 --> 00:17:11,098
Private Clark's mental state
could mean that he's unfit for trial.

252
00:17:11,099 --> 00:17:13,779
Now, that's a possibility, General.

253
00:17:13,819 --> 00:17:17,858
Not much honour there.
Anyway, our trick cyclists say he's sound.

254
00:17:17,859 --> 00:17:20,089
(CLOCK TICKING)

255
00:17:21,139 --> 00:17:23,739
Right. I'll report back.

256
00:17:26,859 --> 00:17:28,849
Gentlemen.

257
00:17:31,619 --> 00:17:33,419
(DOOR OPENS AND CLOSES)

258
00:17:33,459 --> 00:17:37,249
How much of a sacrificial lamb
is this soldier, Alan?

259
00:17:37,299 --> 00:17:41,178
Well, I suppose we could withdraw
HMG's consent to the trial,

260
00:17:41,179 --> 00:17:44,218
although the PM's inclined
to take it on the chin.

261
00:17:44,219 --> 00:17:46,979
It's Private Clark in the dock, not HMG.

262
00:17:47,019 --> 00:17:49,338
Well, it puts clear blue water
between us and the US.

263
00:17:49,339 --> 00:17:53,698
It shows that the British
don't shy away from international justice.

264
00:17:53,699 --> 00:17:59,699
If Deed was on the panel,
swimming our way, so much the better.

265
00:17:59,739 --> 00:18:03,619
How to get Deed to swim
other than against the tide, Alan?

266
00:18:03,659 --> 00:18:05,779
(CLOCK TICKING)

267
00:18:05,819 --> 00:18:09,289
Your name's been put forward
for the trial panel.

268
00:18:09,339 --> 00:18:11,658
The Government feels
it should be represented.

269
00:18:11,659 --> 00:18:13,938
I don't represent the Government, Ian.

270
00:18:13,939 --> 00:18:17,409
His Honour Judge Previn
is resisting your inclusion.

271
00:18:17,459 --> 00:18:19,858
He hasn't got much trial experience.

272
00:18:19,859 --> 00:18:22,978
Do any of them? They'll make up
the deficiency by time-wasting.

273
00:18:22,979 --> 00:18:25,418
Oh, no, that means a year or more, Ian.

274
00:18:25,419 --> 00:18:27,938
I'd really rather
one of the otherjudges did it.

275
00:18:27,939 --> 00:18:34,579
I think there's a move afoot to sacrifice
Private Clark to our exit strategy.

276
00:18:34,619 --> 00:18:36,058
Which is what, exactly?

277
00:18:36,059 --> 00:18:37,859
I'm not privy to it, John,

278
00:18:37,899 --> 00:18:40,538
but I think it involves an English judge

279
00:18:40,539 --> 00:18:44,699
helping to deliverjustice
by finding Private Clark guilty.

280
00:18:44,739 --> 00:18:46,569
And if he's not guilty?

281
00:18:46,619 --> 00:18:49,086
It's a small price to pay, it's felt,

282
00:18:49,099 --> 00:18:52,299
for saving the lives
of British soldiers in Iraq.

283
00:19:44,539 --> 00:19:46,579
(SIGHS)

284
00:19:58,659 --> 00:20:00,699
(SIGHS)

285
00:20:09,099 --> 00:20:14,889
(SIGHS) John, please go
before I say something I might regret.

286
00:20:17,419 --> 00:20:19,409
Just go.

287
00:20:22,459 --> 00:20:24,419
Go!

288
00:20:28,099 --> 00:20:32,489
I can't do the case in The Hague.
Deed's on the trial panel.

289
00:20:32,539 --> 00:20:37,539
- That's madness, Jo.
- We had a relationship, Simon.

290
00:20:37,579 --> 00:20:39,818
Well, then, play him to our advantage.

291
00:20:39,819 --> 00:20:44,059
Think about it, Jo. It's like a gift
to Dave Clark's defence.

292
00:20:53,499 --> 00:20:55,489
(BIRDSONG)

293
00:21:21,699 --> 00:21:23,138
- Goedendag.
- Goedendag.

294
00:21:23,139 --> 00:21:25,579
I'm Dr Pavely. I have a reservation.

295
00:21:25,619 --> 00:21:28,089
Ah, yes, Dr Pavely.

296
00:21:28,139 --> 00:21:31,059
If you could fill out the registration.

297
00:21:33,579 --> 00:21:35,859
Shall I unpack, Judge?

298
00:21:37,259 --> 00:21:40,818
- This trial's reckoned to last a year.
- Are we staying?

299
00:21:40,819 --> 00:21:43,098
Well, from what I've read so far,
we could do it in three weeks.

300
00:21:43,099 --> 00:21:44,499
I'll unpack, then.

301
00:21:44,539 --> 00:21:46,378
Marie Madsen,
chief prosecutor of the ICC.

302
00:21:46,379 --> 00:21:48,378
Excuse me! Mr Justice Deed is busy!

303
00:21:48,379 --> 00:21:51,929
- She's here now, Coop.
- So much for security!

304
00:21:51,979 --> 00:21:55,529
I don't think there's much danger
in Ms Madsen.

305
00:21:55,579 --> 00:21:59,369
You look older
than your reputation led me to expect.

306
00:21:59,419 --> 00:22:01,099
Touché.

307
00:22:03,459 --> 00:22:07,339
- Do you fence?
- Games are a waste of time.

308
00:22:07,379 --> 00:22:10,339
It's mental discipline, like yoga.

309
00:22:10,379 --> 00:22:12,849
- You do yoga?
- No, I'm too lazy.

310
00:22:12,899 --> 00:22:15,858
Have you read the papers
in the Private Clark case?

311
00:22:15,859 --> 00:22:18,459
There is a lot to read.

312
00:22:18,499 --> 00:22:21,619
Well,
we have a drink later and talk, yes?

313
00:22:21,659 --> 00:22:26,449
Is it quite proper? The chief prosecutor
and one of the trial judges?

314
00:22:26,499 --> 00:22:29,458
Ourjoint concern
is the achievement of justice.

315
00:22:29,459 --> 00:22:32,298
I thought that you presented
your evidence, the defence theirs,

316
00:22:32,299 --> 00:22:34,659
and the judges decide.

317
00:22:34,699 --> 00:22:36,738
Article 54 (1 ) says my task
is to investigate

318
00:22:36,739 --> 00:22:39,939
both incriminating
and exonerating circumstances.

319
00:22:41,939 --> 00:22:44,218
You should not hear this case, Sir John.

320
00:22:44,219 --> 00:22:46,129
Until later.

321
00:22:54,459 --> 00:22:57,219
She's a forceful lady.

322
00:22:57,259 --> 00:22:59,329
Bit of a madam, if you ask me.

323
00:22:59,379 --> 00:23:03,579
She has a reputation
for getting drunk with the trial judges.

324
00:23:18,779 --> 00:23:21,539
Oh, I'm a police officer.

325
00:23:21,579 --> 00:23:23,569
Oh.

326
00:23:24,659 --> 00:23:27,019
So, is, er...

327
00:23:27,059 --> 00:23:29,859
is your policewoman a lesbian?

328
00:23:29,899 --> 00:23:32,019
(SNORTS)

329
00:23:32,059 --> 00:23:36,178
You have a reputation
for getting too close to the trial judges.

330
00:23:36,179 --> 00:23:38,859
You have a reputation for interfering.

331
00:23:38,899 --> 00:23:42,129
Isn't that how European courts work?

332
00:23:42,179 --> 00:23:44,738
The evidence points
to Private Clark's guilt.

333
00:23:44,739 --> 00:23:47,378
His defence team would say
it points to lack of legal guilt.

334
00:23:47,379 --> 00:23:51,378
- You're familiar with his defence team?
- My statement didn't imply that.

335
00:23:51,379 --> 00:23:53,446
Do you have sex with Mrs Mills?

336
00:23:54,739 --> 00:23:57,419
You get right to it, don't you?

337
00:23:57,459 --> 00:23:59,769
Ah, you don't deny it.

338
00:23:59,819 --> 00:24:02,939
Am I being cross-examined?

339
00:24:02,979 --> 00:24:08,459
- Oh, how can she defend this man?
- I'm not running the defence.

340
00:24:08,499 --> 00:24:13,289
- It's for us to interrogate him.
- With his defence team.

341
00:24:15,499 --> 00:24:19,618
When I heard you might be on this panel,
I was sure I wouldn't like you.

342
00:24:19,619 --> 00:24:22,929
Hm. Now you've changed
your mind, have you?

343
00:24:22,979 --> 00:24:26,289
You are frank, forthright.

344
00:24:27,339 --> 00:24:32,606
I can't stand men who don't know what
they want, nor how to get it when they do.

345
00:24:35,619 --> 00:24:37,739
Would you like to make love?

346
00:24:37,779 --> 00:24:40,459
I think that would be very nice.

347
00:24:40,499 --> 00:24:43,419
Oh, this is good.

348
00:24:43,459 --> 00:24:47,978
But at this point I must reveal I'm from
the News Of The World and leave.

349
00:24:47,979 --> 00:24:49,619
This is a joke?

350
00:24:49,674 --> 00:24:52,754
Yeah, it's...
it's more a cultural difference.

351
00:24:52,779 --> 00:24:55,580
- I thought you knew what you wanted.
- Yes, I'll regret it all my life.

352
00:24:55,581 --> 00:24:57,372
- (KNOCK AT DOOR)
- Yes...

353
00:24:57,501 --> 00:25:00,731
(CLICKS TONGUE) ...I think you will.

354
00:25:03,341 --> 00:25:05,331
Sir John?

355
00:25:08,301 --> 00:25:10,771
Nick of time.

356
00:25:10,821 --> 00:25:13,541
- (THUNDER ROLLING)
- (ALARM SOUNDING)

357
00:25:14,821 --> 00:25:17,741
(ALARM DROWNS OUT SPEECH)

358
00:25:22,181 --> 00:25:25,048
Are they going to find me guilty,
Mrs Mills?

359
00:25:26,061 --> 00:25:29,101
- Are they?
- We think our case is good.

360
00:25:29,141 --> 00:25:32,981
Dave, I want you to bring
your mother into court.

361
00:25:34,501 --> 00:25:37,891
No. She's not well. She can't.

362
00:25:37,941 --> 00:25:40,380
They'll hear how you helped
look after her when you were growing up.

363
00:25:40,381 --> 00:25:42,300
They'll see her in her wheelchair.

364
00:25:42,301 --> 00:25:45,901
I don't want her to hear
about those children.

365
00:25:45,941 --> 00:25:48,381
No, it... it'll kill her.

366
00:25:49,421 --> 00:25:52,181
Please don't make her come here.

367
00:25:55,781 --> 00:25:57,771
(PANTING)

368
00:26:08,581 --> 00:26:10,621
(PANTING)

369
00:26:14,181 --> 00:26:15,941
I wish I could do that.

370
00:26:15,981 --> 00:26:17,811
Excuse me?

371
00:26:17,861 --> 00:26:20,540
Do you know the fable
of the hare and the tortoise?

372
00:26:20,541 --> 00:26:22,371
I'm sure it ends badly.

373
00:26:22,421 --> 00:26:25,811
- Well...
- She's a guest at our hotel.

374
00:26:25,861 --> 00:26:28,461
They must be giving her different food.

375
00:26:36,701 --> 00:26:38,531
(TRAM BELL RINGS)

376
00:26:38,581 --> 00:26:41,382
- (BOAT SOUNDS HORN)
- (BICYCLE BELL RINGS)

377
00:26:41,421 --> 00:26:43,061
Hi.

378
00:26:43,101 --> 00:26:45,411
I wanted this to be informal.

379
00:26:45,461 --> 00:26:47,851
Not country to country.

380
00:26:47,901 --> 00:26:51,691
You require me to step down.
Marie Madsen told me.

381
00:26:51,741 --> 00:26:54,340
The Iraqis do not wish an English judge
to be on the panel.

382
00:26:54,341 --> 00:26:56,411
No, of course not.

383
00:26:56,461 --> 00:26:59,940
The is a most prominent case, monsieur.
We must accommodate them.

384
00:26:59,941 --> 00:27:01,980
Well, because of its prominence,
we shouldn't.

385
00:27:01,981 --> 00:27:05,380
How could the British continue in Iraq
if we gave in to their demands?

386
00:27:05,381 --> 00:27:08,540
The British shouldn't be in Iraq.
Nor the Americans.

387
00:27:08,541 --> 00:27:12,701
The UK has a commitment,
to help stabilise things.

388
00:27:12,741 --> 00:27:15,180
Can they ever be stable
under an occupying power?

389
00:27:15,181 --> 00:27:18,220
- Will it ever be stable without?
- With anyone else's help, yes.

390
00:27:18,221 --> 00:27:20,691
- Is France helping?
- Naturellement.

391
00:27:20,741 --> 00:27:22,891
Working behind the scenes.

392
00:27:22,941 --> 00:27:24,901
Naturellement.

393
00:27:26,181 --> 00:27:29,980
You'll find my trial experience helpful
in moving things along.

394
00:27:29,981 --> 00:27:32,371
These cases take time, monsieur.

395
00:27:32,421 --> 00:27:35,940
Too much time is being wasted.
We could do this in three weeks.

396
00:27:35,941 --> 00:27:39,021
I am the trial president, monsieur.

397
00:27:42,341 --> 00:27:45,381
- Mrs Mills, Judge.
- (PHONE RINGING)

398
00:27:45,421 --> 00:27:47,700
- Coop...
- I know, Judge, some tea.

399
00:27:47,701 --> 00:27:50,061
No, I haven't come for tea.

400
00:27:52,461 --> 00:27:54,380
You're on the panel
hearing Private Clark.

401
00:27:54,381 --> 00:27:56,300
Somebody doesn't want me to be.

402
00:27:56,301 --> 00:27:59,221
Good. Neither do I.
I want you to step down.

403
00:28:01,181 --> 00:28:04,701
Britain has to be represented.

404
00:28:04,741 --> 00:28:07,340
Since when have you become
the Government's hack?

405
00:28:07,341 --> 00:28:09,251
That's not the issue.

406
00:28:09,301 --> 00:28:11,940
Nor will I allow your new chums
to make it so.

407
00:28:11,941 --> 00:28:13,820
It's our Government
who should be on trial.

408
00:28:13,821 --> 00:28:16,460
Taking that line will damage your career.

409
00:28:16,461 --> 00:28:20,700
My career's just fine, thank you. I'd
sooner not be part of the Establishment.

410
00:28:20,701 --> 00:28:23,940
- Like it or not, Jo, you are.
- I'm increasingly ashamed to be British.

411
00:28:23,941 --> 00:28:27,875
Joining Jumping Jack Flash's set's
not going to help, is it?

412
00:28:30,421 --> 00:28:32,020
I want you to think
about what I've asked.

413
00:28:32,021 --> 00:28:35,101
Yeah, yeah. What defence are you running?

414
00:28:35,141 --> 00:28:38,608
Going after the Government
will not help your client.

415
00:28:42,301 --> 00:28:44,220
Claude says you refuse to step down.

416
00:28:44,221 --> 00:28:46,620
- I'm sorry, Sir John.
- It's all right, she's harmless.

417
00:28:46,621 --> 00:28:50,020
Pah! Islamic terrorists
are harmless compared to me!

418
00:28:50,021 --> 00:28:54,541
It's fine. Marie Madsen, Jo Mills.
Prosecution, meet the defence.

419
00:28:54,581 --> 00:28:58,421
Ah, Mrs Mills. I know about you.

420
00:28:58,461 --> 00:29:02,020
You will step down from this case,
Sir John. It's essential.

421
00:29:02,021 --> 00:29:05,180
- Persuade him, Mrs Mills.
- I don't have that sort of influence.

422
00:29:05,181 --> 00:29:07,621
Then leave. I'll talk to him.

423
00:29:07,661 --> 00:29:11,500
In England, the prosecution and the
defence see the trial judge together.

424
00:29:11,501 --> 00:29:13,891
This is The Hague, Mrs Mills.

425
00:29:13,941 --> 00:29:18,101
Mm. Where it seems
you need little law to get by.

426
00:29:19,941 --> 00:29:21,931
What did she mean?

427
00:29:21,981 --> 00:29:24,620
No matter how differently you do things
here, I won't see you without the defence.

428
00:29:24,621 --> 00:29:28,020
- Because of your relationship?
- I don't answer to you, Marie.

429
00:29:28,021 --> 00:29:31,780
You must withdraw when you have
a relationship with an advocate.

430
00:29:31,781 --> 00:29:33,220
You have a relationship with Previn.

431
00:29:33,221 --> 00:29:35,980
You're in and out of his room
like a fiddler's elbow.

432
00:29:35,981 --> 00:29:38,421
This is not an affair.

433
00:29:38,461 --> 00:29:40,820
What, you simply get drunk
in his room, do you?

434
00:29:40,821 --> 00:29:42,740
- Where did you hear that?
- Maybe you should step down.

435
00:29:42,741 --> 00:29:47,131
Never! I'm going to send
this British soldier to prison.

436
00:29:50,141 --> 00:29:52,131
(PANTING)

437
00:29:59,261 --> 00:30:02,220
This is getting to be a habit,
the hare passing the tortoise.

438
00:30:02,221 --> 00:30:05,621
- The tortoise wins in the end.
- Only in the fable.

439
00:30:12,461 --> 00:30:14,660
Will you be here
after your AIDS conference?

440
00:30:14,661 --> 00:30:16,941
No, I have to go to Switzerland.

441
00:30:16,981 --> 00:30:19,781
Where you run up and down mountains?

442
00:30:19,821 --> 00:30:22,701
(BOTH CHUCKLE)

443
00:30:22,741 --> 00:30:27,531
How about a more leisurely bike ride
around the city?

444
00:30:27,581 --> 00:30:30,621
- I have to do some work.
- Oh, yeah.

445
00:30:30,661 --> 00:30:32,941
That'll be our epitaph, won't it?

446
00:30:32,981 --> 00:30:36,020
Putting life on hold
while we wait for work to stop.

447
00:30:36,021 --> 00:30:38,251
Yes. It's a modern disease.

448
00:30:38,301 --> 00:30:40,941
How about that bike ride, then?

449
00:30:42,301 --> 00:30:44,691
No, I really do have to work.

450
00:30:46,181 --> 00:30:50,020
- How about dinner, then?
- You really are very persistent, Mr Deed.

451
00:30:50,021 --> 00:30:52,381
Well...

452
00:30:52,421 --> 00:30:55,221
- I do try.
- (CHUCKLES)

453
00:30:55,261 --> 00:30:57,171
(CAMERA CLICKS)

454
00:30:57,221 --> 00:31:00,021
COURT OFFICIAL: La cour se lève.

455
00:31:07,021 --> 00:31:11,701
Cour Pénale Internationale.
L'audience est ouverte à dix heures.

456
00:31:11,741 --> 00:31:13,381
Good day to everyone.

457
00:31:13,421 --> 00:31:16,541
I would like to start

458
00:31:16,581 --> 00:31:21,021
by asking the defendant
about conditions in detention.

459
00:31:21,061 --> 00:31:24,741
He has any complaints?
He is receiving the correct food?

460
00:31:24,781 --> 00:31:27,141
He is, Your Honour. No complaints.

461
00:31:27,181 --> 00:31:31,021
Can we read out the
charge to the accused?

462
00:31:31,061 --> 00:31:33,380
May it please Your Honour,
before this is read,

463
00:31:33,381 --> 00:31:37,700
I must challenge the propriety
of Sir John Deed to sit on this panel.

464
00:31:37,701 --> 00:31:39,101
On what grounds?

465
00:31:39,141 --> 00:31:42,008
He has a relationship
with the defence lead.

466
00:31:42,021 --> 00:31:43,660
You are presenting evidence?

467
00:31:43,661 --> 00:31:48,581
I would like to examine His Honour
Sir John and Mrs Mills on this point.

468
00:31:49,621 --> 00:31:54,380
Well, I would suggest my chambers, but
there is always a need for transparency.

469
00:31:54,381 --> 00:31:58,060
Not to proceed will raise the spectre
of bias in this important case.

470
00:31:58,061 --> 00:32:03,011
Sir John and Madame Mills,
would you submit to examination?

471
00:32:03,061 --> 00:32:05,621
This is wholly prejudicial.

472
00:32:07,421 --> 00:32:10,261
This trial cannot be subject to bias.

473
00:32:11,581 --> 00:32:15,340
Madame, do you have any relationship
with any judge on this panel?

474
00:32:15,341 --> 00:32:19,701
I know Mr Justice Deed.
I fenced with him only last week.

475
00:32:19,741 --> 00:32:21,980
Your Honour,
this is to favour her client.

476
00:32:21,981 --> 00:32:27,141
- Does Sir John wish to comment?
- (SIGHS) Not in open court, no.

477
00:32:27,181 --> 00:32:30,248
Under our constitution,
we demand transparency.

478
00:32:32,341 --> 00:32:37,741
Is there a relationship
likely to prejudice this trial?

479
00:32:37,781 --> 00:32:40,381
No, there is not.

480
00:32:40,421 --> 00:32:43,781
Then we should be mindful of Article 67,

481
00:32:43,821 --> 00:32:48,221
which guarantees the defendant
the right to choose his own counsel.

482
00:32:49,741 --> 00:32:52,661
May we continue reading out the charge?

483
00:32:52,701 --> 00:32:54,620
Would your honour
hear a further argument?

484
00:32:54,621 --> 00:32:56,901
Regrettably, no.

485
00:32:56,941 --> 00:32:58,741
I have ruled.

486
00:33:05,421 --> 00:33:08,141
David Patrick Clark,

487
00:33:08,181 --> 00:33:11,781
you are charged
with the war crime of wilful killing,

488
00:33:11,821 --> 00:33:16,981
contrary to the Rome Statute,
Article 8, 2 (b),

489
00:33:17,021 --> 00:33:19,755
that on November 11th last at around 5pm,

490
00:33:19,781 --> 00:33:25,221
in the context of and associated with
an international armed conflict,

491
00:33:25,261 --> 00:33:31,821
you did cause the death by shooting
of Asheer Al-Abul, aged four years,

492
00:33:31,861 --> 00:33:33,900
a person you knew to be protected

493
00:33:33,901 --> 00:33:40,421
under the provisions of the Geneva
Convention of 12th August 1949.

494
00:33:40,461 --> 00:33:43,940
You are further charged
with the war crimes of murder,

495
00:33:43,941 --> 00:33:46,661
contrary to Article 8, 2 (c),

496
00:33:46,701 --> 00:33:51,821
in that you did murder civilians
taking no part in the hostilities.

497
00:33:51,861 --> 00:33:55,781
Namely, Maliki Al-Jamil, aged seven,

498
00:33:55,821 --> 00:33:57,971
Eisa Ali Nasser, nine years old,

499
00:33:58,021 --> 00:34:01,301
Ziyal Ataie,

500
00:34:01,341 --> 00:34:03,251
Amira Hakim,

501
00:34:03,301 --> 00:34:05,261
Hussein Husseini,

502
00:34:05,301 --> 00:34:06,821
Sadia Al-Rahman,

503
00:34:06,861 --> 00:34:08,691
Mohammed Rufi,

504
00:34:08,741 --> 00:34:10,971
Qasim Al-Assam,

505
00:34:11,021 --> 00:34:13,141
Murtaza Nazah.

506
00:34:14,461 --> 00:34:18,701
How do you plead, guilty or not guilty?

507
00:34:20,541 --> 00:34:22,611
Not guilty, sir.

508
00:34:22,661 --> 00:34:25,501
Sit down, monsieur.

509
00:34:25,541 --> 00:34:27,261
Madame Mills.

510
00:34:29,181 --> 00:34:31,660
In support of Private Clark's defence,
we have sought

511
00:34:31,661 --> 00:34:34,140
hospital records from
the Iraqi authority.

512
00:34:34,141 --> 00:34:39,141
These relate to the man that Private Clark
rescued and who died in his arms.

513
00:34:39,181 --> 00:34:42,221
These records aren't forthcoming.

514
00:34:42,261 --> 00:34:43,701
You know they exist?

515
00:34:43,741 --> 00:34:46,140
We believe so, based on evidence
from the defendant.

516
00:34:46,141 --> 00:34:47,420
You did request them?

517
00:34:47,421 --> 00:34:51,131
We did. Not having them
is prejudicial to the defence.

518
00:34:51,181 --> 00:34:52,420
But you knew the trial date.

519
00:34:52,421 --> 00:34:56,811
The prosecution could use
its considerable powers under Article 54

520
00:34:56,861 --> 00:35:03,341
to investigate with the Iraq authority
just why these papers aren't forthcoming.

521
00:35:04,461 --> 00:35:06,371
The man is a fiction.

522
00:35:06,421 --> 00:35:08,381
There are no papers.

523
00:35:08,421 --> 00:35:10,780
I would like you to investigate further
and report back.

524
00:35:10,781 --> 00:35:12,981
This is a matter for the defence.

525
00:35:13,021 --> 00:35:16,460
I'm not here to scout for data
to exonerate the accused.

526
00:35:16,461 --> 00:35:19,780
Your statutory obligation
is to establish the truth.

527
00:35:19,781 --> 00:35:22,171
I want those hospital papers.

528
00:35:22,221 --> 00:35:25,340
I'm sure Mrs Mills
will provide the details for you.

529
00:35:25,341 --> 00:35:28,811
- I'm obliged, My Lord.
- Wrong form, Mrs Mills.

530
00:35:28,861 --> 00:35:30,461
Your Honour.

531
00:35:30,501 --> 00:35:32,491
Can we proceed?

532
00:35:33,781 --> 00:35:37,620
I'd first like to enquire
about the defendant's mental state.

533
00:35:37,621 --> 00:35:40,931
Er... no. The prisoner
was declared fit for trial.

534
00:35:40,981 --> 00:35:43,060
Yes, I read
the Army psychiatrists' reports.

535
00:35:43,061 --> 00:35:45,060
Are there any independent reports?

536
00:35:45,061 --> 00:35:50,128
How many psychies must a man have to see
before they can call him quite sane?

537
00:35:50,661 --> 00:35:53,101
British Army psychiatrists.

538
00:35:53,141 --> 00:35:56,475
- No less qualified.
- The prosecution has a point.

539
00:35:56,501 --> 00:35:57,740
Yes, they work for the Army.

540
00:35:57,741 --> 00:36:01,861
Work cultures affect the minds of us all,
even psychiatrists.

541
00:36:01,901 --> 00:36:04,580
The whole of the British Army is mad,
being in Iraq.

542
00:36:04,581 --> 00:36:08,220
Mrs Mills, was the defendant examined
by any other psychiatrists?

543
00:36:08,221 --> 00:36:10,940
He was independently examined four times.

544
00:36:10,941 --> 00:36:13,742
The reports are on your screens
at tab ten.

545
00:36:18,061 --> 00:36:22,260
- Will you be calling these psychiatrists?
- JO: Er... I will not.

546
00:36:22,261 --> 00:36:26,101
In which case, I'd like to examine
the Army psychiatrists.

547
00:36:26,141 --> 00:36:27,541
For what purpose?

548
00:36:27,581 --> 00:36:31,741
I want to be satisfied
as to the mental state of the defendant.

549
00:36:31,781 --> 00:36:34,011
It isn't disputed by anyone.

550
00:36:34,061 --> 00:36:38,421
I find myself agreeing
with the defence. On this point.

551
00:36:39,621 --> 00:36:41,611
(WHISPERS)

552
00:36:44,861 --> 00:36:47,500
- (WHISPERS) We have no other option.
- No, obviously.

553
00:36:47,501 --> 00:36:50,541
We will withdraw to discuss.

554
00:36:50,581 --> 00:36:52,941
COURT OFFICIAL: La cour se lève.

555
00:36:59,941 --> 00:37:03,651
(SIGHS) He's always so infuriating?

556
00:37:03,701 --> 00:37:05,611
You ain't seen nothing.

557
00:37:05,661 --> 00:37:08,131
(CHATTER OUTSIDE)

558
00:37:08,181 --> 00:37:10,171
Relax, Dave.

559
00:37:12,261 --> 00:37:15,260
- Would you wait outside?
- He's got to be guarded, miss.

560
00:37:15,261 --> 00:37:17,021
Yes. But outside.

561
00:37:17,061 --> 00:37:18,980
No, miss. He needs guarding at all times.

562
00:37:18,981 --> 00:37:21,500
Just leave. We need to speak
to Private Clark alone.

563
00:37:21,501 --> 00:37:22,940
- It's orders.
- No, get out!

564
00:37:22,941 --> 00:37:25,820
Simon, tell the judges the guards
are preventing our client

565
00:37:25,821 --> 00:37:28,755
from mounting his defence.
We can't continue.

566
00:37:30,901 --> 00:37:32,861
Fine.

567
00:37:33,901 --> 00:37:35,891
I'll tell them.

568
00:37:39,821 --> 00:37:43,501
You want to punch these guys,
they're so stupid!

569
00:37:43,541 --> 00:37:45,611
They're quite friendly, really.

570
00:37:45,661 --> 00:37:49,861
Regardless, your defence is yours
until it's given in court.

571
00:37:49,901 --> 00:37:52,701
I'm not mad, Mrs Mills.

572
00:37:52,741 --> 00:37:55,740
Dave, this is a ploy
to stop us going to trial.

573
00:37:55,741 --> 00:37:58,620
- I don't think John...
- He is a British judge, Jo,

574
00:37:58,621 --> 00:38:00,260
working for the Government.

575
00:38:00,261 --> 00:38:04,020
- Why is he asking if I'm mad?
- To try to stop the trial.

576
00:38:04,021 --> 00:38:05,900
He's trying to be fair to you.

577
00:38:05,901 --> 00:38:08,180
They were terrorists I shot.
I know they were.

578
00:38:08,181 --> 00:38:11,860
But unfortunately, Dave,
your only witness died in your arms.

579
00:38:11,861 --> 00:38:13,340
No-one believes he existed.

580
00:38:13,341 --> 00:38:15,811
- He was never found.
- He was there.

581
00:38:15,861 --> 00:38:18,060
Someone's hiding those hospital records.

582
00:38:18,061 --> 00:38:20,260
Can you stop the psychiatrists
saying I'm mad?

583
00:38:20,261 --> 00:38:24,100
- Is that what they're going to say?
- I'm not mad, Mrs Mills.

584
00:38:24,101 --> 00:38:25,701
I'm not.

585
00:38:25,741 --> 00:38:28,860
Does psychiatry not obscure
the waters of criminal trials?

586
00:38:28,861 --> 00:38:32,701
- Is this what you wish, monsieur?
- You know differently.

587
00:38:32,741 --> 00:38:35,381
I can order this not to happen.

588
00:38:38,461 --> 00:38:41,221
Then we'll fall out publicly.

589
00:38:41,261 --> 00:38:45,101
Your government
is in a most unhappy place.

590
00:38:45,141 --> 00:38:49,260
The Iraqis want this atrocity condemned,
and your army is in need of protection.

591
00:38:49,261 --> 00:38:52,101
Madness is a convenient escape.

592
00:38:52,141 --> 00:38:55,341
The psychiatrists may confirm
he's not mad.

593
00:38:55,381 --> 00:38:57,140
My concern is to be convinced

594
00:38:57,141 --> 00:39:00,740
that Clark was in control of his mind
when he shot those people.

595
00:39:00,741 --> 00:39:03,181
Otherwise, the trial cannot proceed.

596
00:39:08,781 --> 00:39:12,420
This could mean the British Army
escaping without punishment.

597
00:39:12,421 --> 00:39:14,488
Is this Judge Deed's intention?

598
00:39:14,501 --> 00:39:17,740
I can think of no other reason
to examine the psychiatrists.

599
00:39:17,741 --> 00:39:21,741
Deed wanted to shorten
the trial time, not extend it.

600
00:39:21,781 --> 00:39:25,141
Then I will ask our Foreign Minister

601
00:39:25,181 --> 00:39:27,940
to complain to the United Nations
about Judge Deed.

602
00:39:27,941 --> 00:39:30,701
Perhaps we can remove him.

603
00:39:30,741 --> 00:39:32,891
MARIE: We can hope.

604
00:39:43,341 --> 00:39:47,261
- Is Deed helping us, Alan?
- For once, he appears to be.

605
00:39:47,301 --> 00:39:52,700
He would help us by denying Mrs Mills and
her cronies their international platform.

606
00:39:52,701 --> 00:39:56,821
We plan to separate Mrs Mills
from her loony friends...

607
00:39:57,821 --> 00:40:00,131
...and undermine their case.

608
00:40:00,181 --> 00:40:01,741
That'll delay things.

609
00:40:01,781 --> 00:40:04,620
I had no idea
he'd try to shorten the trial time.

610
00:40:04,621 --> 00:40:06,851
Your medics could help,

611
00:40:06,901 --> 00:40:10,780
if the Army were to reconsider its
position on Private Clark's mental state.

612
00:40:10,781 --> 00:40:13,460
And how would that help
the situation in Basra?

613
00:40:13,461 --> 00:40:16,140
A speedy resolution
will benefit everyone.

614
00:40:16,141 --> 00:40:18,501
Yes, I suppose.

615
00:40:18,541 --> 00:40:22,901
(SIGHS) Our boys
are having a difficult time.

616
00:40:27,101 --> 00:40:30,701
But your way
so frequently prevails, Marie.

617
00:40:30,741 --> 00:40:34,581
This is such an important trial
for you, Claude.

618
00:40:34,621 --> 00:40:38,141
We must not allow ourselves
to be embarrassed.

619
00:40:38,181 --> 00:40:40,340
Monsieur Deed is right to seek records

620
00:40:40,341 --> 00:40:43,060
that may have come detached
from that dead witness.

621
00:40:43,061 --> 00:40:47,061
How? Iraq is in chaos, Claude,
the result of invading forces.

622
00:40:47,101 --> 00:40:50,061
Then he will insist on the psychiatrists.

623
00:40:53,701 --> 00:40:55,421
Claude.

624
00:40:56,421 --> 00:40:59,061
There is, um...

625
00:40:59,101 --> 00:41:02,251
something I must tell you about Deed.

626
00:41:02,301 --> 00:41:03,701
It's messy.

627
00:41:03,741 --> 00:41:06,581
Then you'd better come to my chambers.

628
00:41:11,261 --> 00:41:12,900
Your taxi is waiting, Doctor.

629
00:41:12,901 --> 00:41:15,740
Can you give this
to Sir John Deed for me, please?

630
00:41:15,741 --> 00:41:17,781
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

631
00:41:22,581 --> 00:41:24,651
(CHATTER)

632
00:41:25,701 --> 00:41:29,620
Answer me, monsieur. Are you still
sexually pressuring Marie Madsen?

633
00:41:29,621 --> 00:41:32,181
Oh, please!

634
00:41:32,221 --> 00:41:35,141
- Monsieur?
- This is all nonsense.

635
00:41:35,181 --> 00:41:37,571
You find women troublesome, no?

636
00:41:37,621 --> 00:41:39,611
But I love them!

637
00:41:42,181 --> 00:41:44,820
- You have such a reputation.
- Evidence, Claude.

638
00:41:44,821 --> 00:41:46,781
Marie is very beautiful, no?

639
00:41:46,821 --> 00:41:50,420
She should try using a little less sex
and a little more law.

640
00:41:50,421 --> 00:41:53,701
I will examine those psychiatrists.

641
00:41:54,861 --> 00:41:59,180
In any dangerous situation,
the normal response is fight or flight.

642
00:41:59,181 --> 00:42:01,741
This is what adrenaline does for us.

643
00:42:01,781 --> 00:42:04,821
In soldiers,
the urge to flee is suppressed,

644
00:42:04,861 --> 00:42:07,941
both by training
and by peer-group pressure.

645
00:42:07,981 --> 00:42:09,581
Soldiers are not normal?

646
00:42:09,621 --> 00:42:13,180
If you accept the norm as not
unquestioningly submitting to discipline,

647
00:42:13,181 --> 00:42:15,741
or subjecting oneself to danger.

648
00:42:15,781 --> 00:42:18,900
Does Private Clark
need to subject himself to danger?

649
00:42:18,901 --> 00:42:21,060
That tendency exists in most soldiers.

650
00:42:21,061 --> 00:42:25,611
Private Clark comes from
a very protected background.

651
00:42:25,661 --> 00:42:30,380
His mother is an invalid and was confined
to a wheelchair soon after his birth.

652
00:42:30,381 --> 00:42:32,460
His older sisters are her carers

653
00:42:32,461 --> 00:42:36,660
and brought up Private Clark
after his father deserted the family.

654
00:42:36,661 --> 00:42:40,541
He has a greater need for danger
than most.

655
00:42:40,581 --> 00:42:44,140
Does that predispose him
to a low threshold of self-control?

656
00:42:44,141 --> 00:42:46,181
In my opinion, yes.

657
00:42:46,221 --> 00:42:51,688
In your opinion, was he beyond reasonable
self-control at the time of the shooting?

658
00:42:55,261 --> 00:42:59,811
On re-examining the hours
of interviews, I'd say he was.

659
00:43:03,981 --> 00:43:05,301
Thank you.

660
00:43:05,621 --> 00:43:08,501
- (LOW CHATTER)
- (LIFT BELL RINGS)

661
00:43:08,541 --> 00:43:11,011
Hi. 312, please.

662
00:43:11,061 --> 00:43:14,181
- (LIFT BELL RINGS)
- Thank you.

663
00:43:14,221 --> 00:43:17,931
- Dr Pavely left this for you, sir.
- Thanks.

664
00:43:22,141 --> 00:43:24,660
- Is she coming back?
- She's kept her room on.

665
00:43:24,661 --> 00:43:27,501
(LAUGHTER)

666
00:43:28,621 --> 00:43:31,380
See if you can get Dr Pavely's number,
will you?

667
00:43:31,381 --> 00:43:34,581
(LAUGHTER AND CHATTER)

668
00:43:34,621 --> 00:43:37,091
COURT OFFICIAL: La cour se lève.

669
00:43:43,181 --> 00:43:45,331
Madame Mills.

670
00:43:46,661 --> 00:43:50,620
In your original opinion, along
with that of Doctors Denman and Smith,

671
00:43:50,621 --> 00:43:55,061
you thought he was "reasoning
and therefore responsible".

672
00:43:55,101 --> 00:43:57,540
Madame, you appear to be working
against your client.

673
00:43:57,541 --> 00:44:00,101
No, these are his instructions.

674
00:44:00,141 --> 00:44:04,501
He won't accept he was in shock
and therefore not responsible.

675
00:44:05,581 --> 00:44:08,660
When the Government
puts a young soldier in the position

676
00:44:08,661 --> 00:44:11,341
that Private Clark was put in,

677
00:44:11,381 --> 00:44:16,621
even with all his training, he is still
subject to human error in extremis.

678
00:44:16,661 --> 00:44:19,340
He found himself in the most
desperate, terrifying situation,

679
00:44:19,341 --> 00:44:23,181
with only a fraction of a second
to respond.

680
00:44:23,221 --> 00:44:29,011
He believes that in killing terrorists
as he did, he exercised properjudgment.

681
00:44:29,061 --> 00:44:31,540
His colonel agreed with that, initially.

682
00:44:31,541 --> 00:44:34,141
I have a copy of Colonel Roth's report.

683
00:44:37,981 --> 00:44:39,581
(WHISPERING)

684
00:44:43,261 --> 00:44:45,460
How was this obtained, Mrs Mills?

685
00:44:45,461 --> 00:44:48,140
It was sent to me anonymously
from Brigade Headquarters.

686
00:44:48,141 --> 00:44:50,380
- The sender is unknown?
- Regrettably.

687
00:44:50,381 --> 00:44:54,460
Perhaps the prosecution can use
its considerable investigatory powers

688
00:44:54,461 --> 00:44:56,341
to identify the sender.

689
00:44:56,381 --> 00:44:58,371
Resume, Madame Mills.

690
00:44:58,421 --> 00:45:02,620
Dr Froad, are psychiatric disorders
prevalent in the soldiers serving in Iraq?

691
00:45:02,621 --> 00:45:05,020
They appear wherever soldiers wage war.

692
00:45:05,021 --> 00:45:10,181
- Particularly so in Iraq?
- There are stress problems.

693
00:45:10,221 --> 00:45:14,141
Isn't it true that out of a force
of approximately 8,000,

694
00:45:14,181 --> 00:45:19,181
over 1,500 have been sent home
suffering psychiatric disorders?

695
00:45:20,741 --> 00:45:22,941
There is a lot of stress.

696
00:45:22,981 --> 00:45:26,021
Is that a yes or a no, Doctor?

697
00:45:26,061 --> 00:45:27,891
Yes.

698
00:45:27,941 --> 00:45:31,620
Was Private Clark sent home
suffering with a psychiatric condition

699
00:45:31,621 --> 00:45:33,981
two months before the shootings,

700
00:45:34,021 --> 00:45:37,088
but then declared fit
for active service again?

701
00:45:40,421 --> 00:45:42,411
Mistakenly, in my opinion.

702
00:45:43,461 --> 00:45:46,860
But your original opinion
when you saw him back in Iraq

703
00:45:46,861 --> 00:45:49,728
had been that he was fit
for active service.

704
00:45:51,141 --> 00:45:55,141
It was, but on reflection,
I was wrong in that.

705
00:45:56,141 --> 00:45:59,691
Has pressure been put on you
to change that opinion?

706
00:46:00,741 --> 00:46:03,275
- We did a longer examination.
- I see.

707
00:46:04,421 --> 00:46:05,821
Doctor.

708
00:46:05,861 --> 00:46:09,571
Are you now saying
that Private Clark was so stressed

709
00:46:09,621 --> 00:46:12,580
that he wasn't responsible
for these killings?

710
00:46:12,581 --> 00:46:18,371
I am saying his actions were simply
a reflex. We say he was "automated".

711
00:46:18,421 --> 00:46:20,381
- No controlling mind.
- No!

712
00:46:20,421 --> 00:46:22,491
No, that's not right.

713
00:46:25,301 --> 00:46:27,780
Sir Joseph Channing's clerk rang, Judge.

714
00:46:27,781 --> 00:46:30,900
She's looking for a date to review
the Shelby Brookes appeal.

715
00:46:30,901 --> 00:46:32,580
What's our timetable here?

716
00:46:32,581 --> 00:46:35,980
Well, Judge Previn has to go to Paris
tomorrow, so tomorrow's possible.

717
00:46:35,981 --> 00:46:37,381
OK, fine.

718
00:46:46,021 --> 00:46:49,901
I somehow feel we're letting
Private Clark down, badly.

719
00:46:52,861 --> 00:46:56,141
He's very unhappy
about those psychiatrists.

720
00:46:58,221 --> 00:47:01,451
A guilty plea would be
more honourable, sir.

721
00:47:03,541 --> 00:47:05,531
Yeah.

722
00:47:20,501 --> 00:47:22,580
I can't go along
with quashing this conviction.

723
00:47:22,581 --> 00:47:26,660
The man was at a political rally
being deliberately provocative.

724
00:47:26,661 --> 00:47:29,380
Urging people to "take action
like proper citizens"?!

725
00:47:29,381 --> 00:47:31,981
It was rather vague.

726
00:47:32,021 --> 00:47:34,860
Brookes was careful to avoid
concrete threats,

727
00:47:34,861 --> 00:47:38,381
while exercising
his democratic right to argue

728
00:47:38,421 --> 00:47:40,940
that Mohammed was backward and primitive.

729
00:47:40,941 --> 00:47:42,380
Who's to say he's not right,

730
00:47:42,381 --> 00:47:46,661
when one considers how Islam
treats women and homosexuals?

731
00:47:46,701 --> 00:47:48,220
That's hardly relevant, anyway,

732
00:47:48,221 --> 00:47:51,260
as much the same could be said
of Roman Catholicism or Judaism.

733
00:47:51,261 --> 00:47:54,541
The mens rea in this
offence is intention.

734
00:47:54,581 --> 00:47:57,381
A conviction must be based on proof

735
00:47:57,421 --> 00:48:02,341
that Brookes deliberately intended
to incite hatred against religion.

736
00:48:02,381 --> 00:48:05,140
Yes, intention is very difficult
to define in law,

737
00:48:05,141 --> 00:48:07,180
but I'm of the view
that that was his intention.

738
00:48:07,181 --> 00:48:10,220
No. That's where
the original trial judge went wrong.

739
00:48:10,221 --> 00:48:13,821
He failed to allow the jury
to consider this

740
00:48:13,861 --> 00:48:17,300
as something primarily subjective,
as the law requires.

741
00:48:17,301 --> 00:48:19,131
Not that I blame him.

742
00:48:19,181 --> 00:48:21,940
I mean, the legislation is
the usual bugger's muddle nowadays.

743
00:48:21,941 --> 00:48:24,340
- (CLOCK CHIMES)
- I'm with Monty on this.

744
00:48:24,341 --> 00:48:25,980
No-one was being attacked in the speech

745
00:48:25,981 --> 00:48:30,141
or reviled for being something
he cannot change, like his race.

746
00:48:30,181 --> 00:48:34,341
But criticism of his ideas
and religion must be allowed,

747
00:48:34,381 --> 00:48:37,420
even if those criticisms
are harsh and aggressive.

748
00:48:37,421 --> 00:48:42,861
I mean, we can lampoon Roman Catholicism
without fear of death threats.

749
00:48:42,901 --> 00:48:48,581
No, Section 29 (j)
of the Public Order Act makes clear

750
00:48:48,621 --> 00:48:52,741
that it was not intended
to prohibit religious criticism,

751
00:48:52,781 --> 00:48:56,981
or even the expression
of abuse or insults.

752
00:48:57,021 --> 00:48:58,700
The conclusion's the same, Joe.

753
00:48:58,701 --> 00:49:00,851
Think about it, John.

754
00:49:01,901 --> 00:49:04,021
(GAVEL BANGING)

755
00:49:04,061 --> 00:49:06,451
All rise.

756
00:49:21,741 --> 00:49:24,701
It is the view of this court

757
00:49:24,741 --> 00:49:30,771
that the judge at Mr Brookes's
original trial failed to direct the jury

758
00:49:30,821 --> 00:49:35,340
to the protections provided in
Section 29 (j) of the Public Order Act.

759
00:49:35,341 --> 00:49:38,621
The wording is very clear.

760
00:49:38,661 --> 00:49:45,061
It specifically allows the expression of,
and let me quote ipsissima verba,

761
00:49:45,101 --> 00:49:51,181
"dislike, ridicule, insult
or abuse of particular religions,

762
00:49:51,221 --> 00:49:55,691
"or the beliefs or practices
of the adherents."

763
00:49:55,741 --> 00:50:00,501
Now, there is, in a free society,
an important difference between that

764
00:50:00,541 --> 00:50:07,341
and the intentional stirring-up of hatred
against people on religious grounds.

765
00:50:07,381 --> 00:50:13,621
That difference was not made clear
adequately to the jury.

766
00:50:13,661 --> 00:50:16,811
Therefore, we quash the conviction.

767
00:50:16,861 --> 00:50:21,491
- (LOUD DISSENT FROM GALLERY)
- The appellant may be released.

768
00:50:21,541 --> 00:50:25,541
We shall rise
whilst the public gallery is cleared.

769
00:50:35,101 --> 00:50:37,380
Isn't there a London contact
for Dr Pavely?

770
00:50:37,381 --> 00:50:39,900
- Only the number the hotel has.
- It's never switched on.

771
00:50:39,901 --> 00:50:42,620
I could check some other sources,
Sir John.

772
00:50:42,621 --> 00:50:45,461
(MAN TALKING ON RADIO)

773
00:50:53,901 --> 00:50:56,541
- (BIRDSONG)
- (DOG BARKS)

774
00:51:04,341 --> 00:51:08,381
- What happened yesterday?
- Last-minute changes.

775
00:51:08,421 --> 00:51:10,811
- I had no contact.
- No.

776
00:51:10,861 --> 00:51:13,421
- Do you have them?
- Of course.

777
00:51:13,461 --> 00:51:15,821
So small, it only kills the target.

778
00:51:15,861 --> 00:51:19,011
They'll trip as soon
as the box is opened.

779
00:51:19,061 --> 00:51:21,781
Fat old men love chocolate.

780
00:51:28,141 --> 00:51:30,660
You've made excellent contact
with our enemy.

781
00:51:30,661 --> 00:51:33,021
I was happy to do this for you.

782
00:51:33,061 --> 00:51:36,371
Your impeccable credentials
weren't questioned.

783
00:51:36,421 --> 00:51:39,221
He seems such a reasonable man.

784
00:51:39,261 --> 00:51:42,060
That's how treacherously deceptive
Western justice is.

785
00:51:42,061 --> 00:51:43,461
Hm.

786
00:51:43,501 --> 00:51:46,581
They smile while they kill your brothers.

787
00:51:46,621 --> 00:51:49,771
- Has something happened to my brothers?
- No.

788
00:51:49,821 --> 00:51:51,651
Contact has been made.

789
00:51:51,701 --> 00:51:55,381
They are training in Afghanistan
with the mujaheddin.

790
00:51:57,861 --> 00:52:02,181
Their cause is just, sister.
But we can bring them back for you.

791
00:52:02,221 --> 00:52:05,355
First, there's something else
we require of you.

792
00:52:27,021 --> 00:52:29,221
Hello, Dave.

793
00:52:32,781 --> 00:52:34,181
How are you?

794
00:52:34,221 --> 00:52:37,500
- Margaret, how did you get...?
- As part of your defence team.

795
00:52:37,501 --> 00:52:40,501
Mark wanted to have a word.

796
00:52:41,741 --> 00:52:44,821
- They treating you right?
- Can't complain.

797
00:52:47,101 --> 00:52:49,981
No, you were never a complainer.

798
00:52:51,261 --> 00:52:53,731
We all admired you for that.

799
00:52:56,061 --> 00:52:58,291
How's things with the rags?

800
00:52:58,341 --> 00:53:00,861
(SIGHS) Bad and getting worse.

801
00:53:01,861 --> 00:53:05,571
The plan by the British Army
and the British Government

802
00:53:05,621 --> 00:53:12,101
is to undermine Private Clark's defence
and so deny him his day in court.

803
00:53:12,141 --> 00:53:18,781
They are trying to recategorise Private
Clark as mad so he can't stand trial.

804
00:53:18,821 --> 00:53:22,660
It is members of the British Government
who should be tried as war criminals,

805
00:53:22,661 --> 00:53:24,971
not Private Clark.

806
00:53:25,021 --> 00:53:31,701
Our Government should be on trial
for persisting in this illegal war.

807
00:53:31,741 --> 00:53:35,421
- A war which, I might add...
- What is going on?

808
00:53:35,461 --> 00:53:38,020
How will this help our client?
We'll be reported to the Bar Council.

809
00:53:38,021 --> 00:53:40,020
It cuts the ground from under them, Jo.

810
00:53:40,021 --> 00:53:43,571
SIMON: ...nor the international community
as a whole.

811
00:53:43,621 --> 00:53:46,380
Do you realise
what your cronies have done?

812
00:53:46,381 --> 00:53:48,691
I think it was justified.

813
00:53:48,741 --> 00:53:50,740
Are you as warped as they are?

814
00:53:50,741 --> 00:53:53,380
You're the leader,
you're going to have to take the rap.

815
00:53:53,381 --> 00:53:55,460
You will get reported to the Bar Council.

816
00:53:55,461 --> 00:53:57,740
It's just another way for the
Government to put pressure on us.

817
00:53:57,741 --> 00:54:00,500
Listen to you!
You're even beginning to sound like them.

818
00:54:00,501 --> 00:54:03,461
- You're out to stop this trial.
- No.

819
00:54:03,501 --> 00:54:05,621
Defect of mind.

820
00:54:05,661 --> 00:54:09,180
If he went not guilty on failure
to appreciate unlawfulness,

821
00:54:09,181 --> 00:54:10,582
he might have walked.

822
00:54:10,621 --> 00:54:14,171
He was sound
when he was sent back to Iraq.

823
00:54:15,221 --> 00:54:18,740
- I was just trying to help you.
- We want our day in court.

824
00:54:18,741 --> 00:54:21,860
Are you out to score political points
or help your client?

825
00:54:21,861 --> 00:54:24,251
- That is pretty cheap.
- (CHUCKLES)

826
00:54:24,301 --> 00:54:29,501
You may not like me joining this set,
but we never compromise our clients.

827
00:54:29,541 --> 00:54:33,461
Their interests and your client's
don't coincide.

828
00:54:33,501 --> 00:54:35,891
They coincide exactly.

829
00:54:35,941 --> 00:54:38,700
Private Clark was sent to do
a job he was trained for.

830
00:54:38,701 --> 00:54:40,140
He made a judgment call

831
00:54:40,141 --> 00:54:43,580
that doesn't accord with the shifting
sands of the Government's war policy.

832
00:54:43,581 --> 00:54:45,180
That does not make him guilty.

833
00:54:45,181 --> 00:54:50,051
He emptied a whole magazine into a
group of civilians, including children.

834
00:54:50,101 --> 00:54:52,580
- 30 bullets.
- Mm-hm. In four seconds.

835
00:54:52,581 --> 00:54:54,540
Well, that's hardly
a split-second decision.

836
00:54:54,541 --> 00:54:58,260
But children made objects of war
can kill as easily as their parents can.

837
00:54:58,261 --> 00:55:00,491
No, that argument's unwinnable.

838
00:55:00,541 --> 00:55:03,141
Well, it would be before a jury.

839
00:55:04,461 --> 00:55:06,501
Defect of mind.

840
00:55:06,541 --> 00:55:09,981
The court could request you
to enter that plea.

841
00:55:10,021 --> 00:55:14,140
That would make my client a scapegoat
and get your employers off the hook.

842
00:55:14,141 --> 00:55:19,661
The Government does not employ me.
Why would I support such a position?

843
00:55:19,701 --> 00:55:22,141
Yes, John...

844
00:55:22,181 --> 00:55:24,171
why would you?

845
00:55:32,981 --> 00:55:34,980
- That seemed rather tense...
- Sorry.

846
00:55:34,981 --> 00:55:39,582
No, it's all right, it's all right,
she's here now. Well, you're back.

847
00:55:39,621 --> 00:55:40,821
Uh-huh.

848
00:55:40,861 --> 00:55:42,860
I tried calling you.
How long are you back for?

849
00:55:42,861 --> 00:55:45,662
Until I get the AIDS
conference organised.

850
00:55:45,701 --> 00:55:50,060
We need the pharmaceutical companies
to donate more drugs. Urgently, you know?

851
00:55:50,061 --> 00:55:53,501
Yep. You better try blackmail.

852
00:55:53,541 --> 00:55:56,140
Remembering to stay
just the side of what's legal.

853
00:55:56,141 --> 00:55:59,221
Oh, yes, where is that? Huh?

854
00:55:59,261 --> 00:56:02,928
Well, we could have dinner
and work out precisely where.

855
00:56:03,541 --> 00:56:05,851
Is that work...

856
00:56:05,901 --> 00:56:08,131
or social?

857
00:56:08,181 --> 00:56:10,461
It's whatever we decide it is.

858
00:56:18,141 --> 00:56:20,131
(BIRDSONG)

859
00:56:28,581 --> 00:56:30,571
(LIFT BELL RINGS)

860
00:56:45,141 --> 00:56:47,181
(BIRDSONG OUTSIDE)

861
00:56:57,901 --> 00:56:59,891
(BEEPING)

862
00:57:09,701 --> 00:57:12,091
(SNIFFS)

863
00:57:12,141 --> 00:57:14,421
Aah!

864
00:57:15,861 --> 00:57:18,941
(GRUNTS AND SOBS)

865
00:57:26,181 --> 00:57:28,171
(SOBBING)

866
00:57:43,061 --> 00:57:45,101
(SOBBING)

